.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt UPDATE 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm update
.Nd periodically write delayed file system writes to the disk
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar seconds
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The file system keeps a regular file's data, and the size, times and block
pointers of its inode, in memory for a while after a
.Xr write 2 ,
writing them to the disk later and together.
.Nm
sets the longest that can be: it detaches into the background and calls
.Xr sync 2
every 30 seconds, so a crash loses at most about that much recent file content.
The file system's structure (names, links, the allocation bitmap) is always
written at once, and is never lost.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl i Ar seconds
Synchronize every
.Ar seconds
seconds instead of 30.
.El
.Pp
.Xr rc 8
starts
.Nm
at boot when
.Va update_enable
is set in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
as it is by default.
It records its process ID in
.Pa /var/run/update.pid .
Single-user mode doesn't start it: delayed writes then reach the disk at
.Xr sync 8 ,
when memory is needed, or at
.Xr reboot 8 .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/run/update.pid -compact
.It Pa /var/run/update.pid
The running daemon's process ID.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sync 2 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr reboot 8 ,
.Xr sync 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
daemon ran on early
.Ux
systems;
.Bx 4.4
moved its job into the kernel's syncer process.
